1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,960
I went to store and bought book.

2
00:00:02,180 --> 00:00:04,900
Book was expensive, but store was crowded.

3
00:00:05,709 --> 00:00:09,369
If English is your first language,
that probably sounds wrong.

4
00:00:09,589 --> 00:00:12,129
But you understood every word of it.

5
00:00:12,655 --> 00:00:15,315
All that's missing are three tiny words.

6
00:00:15,636 --> 00:00:16,534
A.

7
00:00:16,614 --> 00:00:18,457
An. And the.

8
00:00:19,057 --> 00:00:22,417
The word "the" is the most common
word in the English language.

9
00:00:22,757 --> 00:00:24,957
"A" is in the top five.

10
00:00:25,576 --> 00:00:29,536
And yet roughly a third of the world's
languages have no articles at all.

11
00:00:30,516 --> 00:00:35,856
Korean, Hindi, Finnish, Polish, Czech and
many others get by without a single one.

12
00:00:36,362 --> 00:00:38,102
So why does English bother?

13
00:00:38,762 --> 00:00:41,262
And what would happen if
we simply got rid of them?

14
00:00:43,662 --> 00:00:47,242
Let's start with something
simple. I saw a dog.

15
00:00:47,663 --> 00:00:49,023
I saw the dog.

16
00:00:49,850 --> 00:00:52,010
There's a dog in both sentences.

17
00:00:52,870 --> 00:00:54,630
The difference isn't about the dog.

18
00:00:55,150 --> 00:00:57,530
It's about what I assume you already know.

19
00:00:58,099 --> 00:01:01,759
"A dog" is new. We haven't
established which one.

20
00:01:02,019 --> 00:01:04,919
"The dog" is one you
should be able to pick out.

21
00:01:05,602 --> 00:01:07,502
Maybe I mentioned it before.

22
00:01:08,102 --> 00:01:10,102
Maybe we're both looking at it.

23
00:01:10,482 --> 00:01:12,442
Maybe it's the only dog around.

24
00:01:12,790 --> 00:01:15,150
So articles don't tell you what exists.

25
00:01:15,650 --> 00:01:17,510
They tell you how to find it.

26
00:01:19,930 --> 00:01:21,630
Now let's take them away.

27
00:01:22,210 --> 00:01:24,030
Yesterday I went to supermarket.

28
00:01:24,330 --> 00:01:26,450
I bought bottle of wine and some bread.

29
00:01:26,890 --> 00:01:28,910
Wine was surprisingly good.

30
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:33,220
It sounds strange. But what
did you actually misunderstand?

31
00:01:34,072 --> 00:01:35,292
Probably nothing.

32
00:01:36,078 --> 00:01:40,458
You rebuilt the missing pieces
automatically. There was a supermarket.

33
00:01:40,798 --> 00:01:45,058
There was a bottle. And the wine
at the end is the wine I bought.

34
00:01:45,778 --> 00:01:47,618
But notice what you just did.

35
00:01:48,118 --> 00:01:51,238
You worked out what was new
and what was already mentioned.

36
00:01:51,999 --> 00:01:54,119
That's the real job of articles.

37
00:01:54,779 --> 00:01:58,159
They keep track of what the speaker
and the listener already share.

38
00:01:58,860 --> 00:02:00,140
Is this new?

39
00:02:00,491 --> 00:02:04,151
Have we mentioned it? Is it
obvious from the situation?

40
00:02:05,112 --> 00:02:08,072
English answers all of
that with one compact tool.

41
00:02:08,152 --> 00:02:11,932
But it isn't the only
tool. Not even in English.

42
00:02:14,372 --> 00:02:19,332
English often skips articles itself.
I like music. Water is important.

43
00:02:19,612 --> 00:02:21,152
Dogs are intelligent.

44
00:02:21,915 --> 00:02:23,755
No "a". No "the".

45
00:02:23,835 --> 00:02:26,095
Linguists call this the zero article.

46
00:02:26,434 --> 00:02:31,114
And sometimes another word is already
doing the job. I bought my book.

47
00:02:31,634 --> 00:02:33,394
Nobody says "my the book".

48
00:02:34,065 --> 00:02:37,525
"My" tells you whose book.
"One" tells you how many.

49
00:02:37,945 --> 00:02:41,785
"Some" leaves the amount
open. "This" tells you which.

50
00:02:42,044 --> 00:02:45,584
So English has several ways to
tell you how to read a noun.

51
00:02:46,224 --> 00:02:48,184
Articles are just one of them.

52
00:02:48,313 --> 00:02:50,433
And the rules aren't even consistent.

53
00:02:51,113 --> 00:02:52,693
"A dog has four legs."

54
00:02:53,073 --> 00:02:55,973
That's not one new dog.
That's dogs in general.

55
00:02:56,584 --> 00:02:58,844
"The lion is a dangerous animal."

56
00:02:59,484 --> 00:03:03,484
Not a lion you're supposed to
recognise. Lions in general.

57
00:03:03,814 --> 00:03:05,654
So the same little
words can mean something

58
00:03:05,884 --> 00:03:08,954
new, something known, or
a whole kind of thing.

59
00:03:09,121 --> 00:03:11,761
Which is why articles
are so hard to learn.

60
00:03:12,481 --> 00:03:15,561
For speakers of Czech,
Chinese or Japanese, they're

61
00:03:15,641 --> 00:03:18,281
often one of the last
things to fall into place.

62
00:03:20,901 --> 00:03:22,101
Take Czech.

63
00:03:22,402 --> 00:03:24,582
Here's "Yesterday I saw a dog.

64
00:03:24,942 --> 00:03:26,782
The dog ran across the park."

65
00:03:27,449 --> 00:03:30,209
Translate it back word for
word, and you get this.

66
00:03:30,569 --> 00:03:32,649
Yesterday I saw dog.

67
00:03:33,029 --> 00:03:34,609
Dog ran across park.

68
00:03:35,357 --> 00:03:38,237
Exactly the broken
English we started with.

69
00:03:38,717 --> 00:03:40,997
Yet in Czech, it's perfectly clear.

70
00:03:41,663 --> 00:03:44,003
Context tells you it's the same dog.

71
00:03:44,723 --> 00:03:48,183
And when context isn't enough,
Czech reaches for the other tools.

72
00:03:48,929 --> 00:03:50,129
My dog.

73
00:03:50,284 --> 00:03:51,484
One dog.

74
00:03:51,808 --> 00:03:53,448
Some dog or other.

75
00:03:53,866 --> 00:03:55,066
That dog.

76
00:03:55,562 --> 00:03:58,002
Every one of those jobs still gets done.

77
00:03:58,582 --> 00:04:01,442
There's just no single
word dedicated to it.

78
00:04:02,156 --> 00:04:03,996
Czech can even use word order.

79
00:04:04,536 --> 00:04:07,576
Put a man at the end of the
sentence, and he's usually news.

80
00:04:07,876 --> 00:04:11,536
Put him at the start, and he's
usually someone we already know.

81
00:04:13,996 --> 00:04:16,596
So can English survive without articles?

82
00:04:17,256 --> 00:04:18,816
Let's push a little harder.

83
00:04:19,492 --> 00:04:22,512
I went to supermarket and
saw man talking to woman.

84
00:04:22,612 --> 00:04:24,532
Woman gave man key.

85
00:04:25,072 --> 00:04:26,812
Man opened door with key.

86
00:04:27,464 --> 00:04:29,924
You can follow it. But now you're working.

87
00:04:30,232 --> 00:04:31,432
Which door?

88
00:04:31,590 --> 00:04:34,110
One we've seen before, or just some door?

89
00:04:34,650 --> 00:04:37,010
Is that the same woman, or another one?

90
00:04:37,533 --> 00:04:38,933
Now with articles.

91
00:04:39,693 --> 00:04:42,933
I went to the supermarket and
saw a man talking to a woman.

92
00:04:43,540 --> 00:04:45,340
The woman gave the man a key.

93
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:47,880
The man opened the door with the key.

94
00:04:48,588 --> 00:04:50,728
Every "a" brings something new in.

95
00:04:51,148 --> 00:04:53,348
Every "the" tells you which one I mean.

96
00:04:53,976 --> 00:04:57,816
Pronouns help. Word order
helps. Context helps.

97
00:04:57,896 --> 00:05:01,056
But articles make the nouns easy to track.

98
00:05:01,671 --> 00:05:05,911
A tiny amount of information,
placed exactly where you need it.

99
00:05:05,996 --> 00:05:08,596
So English without articles would work.

100
00:05:09,236 --> 00:05:11,036
It would just make you work harder.

101
00:05:12,169 --> 00:05:14,229
Which raises a better question.

102
00:05:14,929 --> 00:05:18,509
If articles are this useful, why
doesn't every language have them?

103
00:05:18,672 --> 00:05:21,012
And where did they come
from in the first place?

104
00:05:23,752 --> 00:05:28,012
About a thousand years ago, English
didn't use them the way it does today.

105
00:05:28,606 --> 00:05:30,846
"The" grew out of a
word that meant "that".

106
00:05:31,366 --> 00:05:33,426
"A" grew out of the word for "one".

107
00:05:34,037 --> 00:05:35,897
In fact, "an" came first.

108
00:05:36,557 --> 00:05:39,797
The "n" fell away before
consonants, and "a" was left.

109
00:05:40,092 --> 00:05:42,492
And sometimes the "n" wandered off.

110
00:05:43,052 --> 00:05:44,992
An apron used to be "a napron".

111
00:05:45,472 --> 00:05:46,972
An adder used to be "a nadder".

112
00:05:47,702 --> 00:05:51,702
People heard the "n" as part of
the article, and it jumped across.

113
00:05:52,491 --> 00:05:54,391
The shift itself makes sense.

114
00:05:55,331 --> 00:05:57,811
"One dog" picks out one member of a group.

115
00:05:58,351 --> 00:06:00,571
Use it often enough,
and it stops counting.

116
00:06:01,271 --> 00:06:03,211
It just introduces something new.

117
00:06:03,414 --> 00:06:06,214
"That dog" points at
something you can identify.

118
00:06:06,734 --> 00:06:09,354
Use it everywhere, and it stops pointing.

119
00:06:10,154 --> 00:06:12,334
It just marks something you can identify.

120
00:06:13,306 --> 00:06:15,676
Linguists call this grammaticalisation.

121
00:06:16,046 --> 00:06:19,806
An ordinary word slowly turns
into a piece of grammar.

122
00:06:20,687 --> 00:06:22,927
And it has happened more than once.

123
00:06:23,307 --> 00:06:24,827
Latin had no articles.

124
00:06:25,356 --> 00:06:29,196
But its daughters do.
French, Spanish, Italian.

125
00:06:29,736 --> 00:06:31,716
And they built them
from the same material.

126
00:06:32,713 --> 00:06:35,013
Their "the" came from the
Latin word for "that".

127
00:06:35,293 --> 00:06:38,133
Their "a" came from the
Latin word for "one".

128
00:06:38,274 --> 00:06:41,494
So articles aren't fundamental
particles of language.

129
00:06:41,674 --> 00:06:44,494
They're old words that got a new job.

130
00:06:45,492 --> 00:06:48,772
And languages don't all put
that job in the same place.

131
00:06:49,449 --> 00:06:51,749
In Swedish, "a car" is two words.

132
00:06:52,009 --> 00:06:53,629
"The car" is one.

133
00:06:53,849 --> 00:06:56,509
The word for car, with
"the" stuck on the end.

134
00:06:57,101 --> 00:07:00,321
Norwegian does the same, and
then goes one step further.

135
00:07:00,941 --> 00:07:03,121
"My book" is usually "boka mi".

136
00:07:03,201 --> 00:07:04,821
Literally, "the book my".

137
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:07,040
Remember "my the book"?

138
00:07:07,480 --> 00:07:09,320
Nobody says that in English.

139
00:07:09,960 --> 00:07:13,180
In Norwegian, it's more or
less the normal way to say it.

140
00:07:13,685 --> 00:07:16,145
Bulgarian puts "the" on the end too.

141
00:07:16,785 --> 00:07:19,825
And Bulgarian is a Slavic
language, just like Czech.

142
00:07:20,503 --> 00:07:23,463
Bulgarian grew an article. Czech didn't.

143
00:07:23,543 --> 00:07:26,703
And neither did most of
its Slavic relatives.

144
00:07:27,343 --> 00:07:29,223
Same problem, different solution.

145
00:07:29,656 --> 00:07:32,116
So neither language is missing anything.

146
00:07:32,596 --> 00:07:35,316
They just keep the information
in different places.

147
00:07:38,176 --> 00:07:42,256
To an English speaker, "I went to store
and bought book" feels incomplete.

148
00:07:43,178 --> 00:07:45,658
But if you speak a language
that doesn't have articles,

149
00:07:46,078 --> 00:07:48,418
you might hear something
completely different.

150
00:07:48,918 --> 00:07:52,058
Maybe it doesn't sound
like broken English at all.

151
00:07:52,398 --> 00:07:56,558
Maybe it sounds like English without
all those annoying little complications.

152
00:07:57,593 --> 00:07:59,333
Neither is more logical.

153
00:07:59,933 --> 00:08:02,453
And grammar isn't a
list of arbitrary rules.

154
00:08:03,238 --> 00:08:05,938
It's a set of different
answers to the same question.

155
00:08:06,683 --> 00:08:09,953
How can one human mind get
another to imagine the same thing?
